.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a minimal keyboard-driven window manager for X. Each window is assigned
to the first available tag and maximised in the usable screen area. Floating
windows are centred and cannot be moved or resized by dragging.
.P
Closing a window compacts the remaining windows into the lowest available tags.
The displayed tag is preserved when possible; if the last occupied tag is
removed, the new last occupied tag is displayed. The configured number of tags
therefore determines the number of windows that can each have a unique tag.
.P
Each screen contains a small status bar which displays the total number of open
windows in brackets, the title of the focused window, and the text read from the
root window name property if the screen is focused. Tag labels are hidden. A
floating window is indicated with a square before the window title.
.P
dwm draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.B X root window name
is read and displayed in the status text area. It can be set with the
.BR xsetroot (1)
command.
.SS Keyboard commands
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-Return
Start
.BR st(1).
.TP
.B Mod1\-p
Spawn
.BR dmenu(1)
for launching other programs.
.TP
.B Mod1\-,
Focus previous screen, if any.
.TP
.B Mod1\-.
Focus next screen, if any.
.TP
.B Mod1\-b
Toggles bar on and off.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-c
Close focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-space
Toggle the focused window between maximised and centred floating states.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Tab
Cycle through open windows while Mod1 is held, then display the selected
window's tag when Mod1 is released.
.TP
.B Mod1\-[1..n]
View the window assigned to the nth tag.
.TP
.B Mod1\-o
Display the tag assigned to the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Shift\-q
Quit dwm.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
dwm is customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the source
code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR st (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
Send all bug reports with a patch to hackers@suckless.org.
